    id: "26ec2afa5d734ee077f6",
    cat: "geopolitica",
    title: "Zambia's president sworn in for second term while opponent detained",
    summary: "Hakainde Hichilema swept to victory, but his main rival is now in custody charged with treason.",
    preview: "Zambia's President Hakainde Hichilema has been sworn in for a second five-year term following a disputed election and the arrest of his main opponent on treason charges. Hichilema - credited with stabilising the economy - swept to victory on 13 August with 60% of the vote, according to official results. Runner-up Brian Mundubile, who received 38%, alleged that the outcome had been tampered with. He was unable to mount a legal challenge to the result as the courts were sealed off last week on the final day a case could be filed.",
    body: "Zambia's President Hakainde Hichilema has been sworn in for a second five-year term following a disputed election and the arrest of his main opponent on treason charges.\n\nHichilema - credited with stabilising the economy - swept to victory on 13 August with 60% of the vote, according to official results. Runner-up Brian Mundubile, who received 38%, alleged that the outcome had been tampered with.\n\nHe was unable to mount a legal challenge to the result as the courts were sealed off last week on the final day a case could be filed.\n\nElection observers said that while polling day was largely peaceful, some have raised questions about the vote tallying process.\n\nEuropean Union observers said staff in many tallying centres did not record results on tally sheets as soon as they were announced and cited weak verification between digital entries and paper records.\n\nIt added that returning officers frequently paused proceedings while awaiting instructions from headquarters.\n\nThe day after the poll, security forces raided a property in the capital, Lusaka, linked to Mundubile, killing a former cabinet minister and arresting a number of opposition politicians.\n\nThe authorities accused the opposition of planning an armed insurrection, which they denied.\n\nMundubile and his running mate, Makebi Zulu, had gone into hiding. They were detained by police last week and charged at the weekend.\n\nDuring his inauguration speech on Tuesday, Hichilema did not comment on his opponent's detention but said that the \"pursuit of political power must never come at the expense of peace, security and stability in our country\".\n\n\"Democracy does not require us to agree on everything. It requires us to respect one another even when we disagree,\" Hichilema added.\n\nHe also invited the opposition to work with his government for the good of the country: \"When you have a good idea, we will listen to you.\"\n\nHe was sworn into office amid jubilant scenes, as thousands of supporters gathered to celebrate the inauguration. His running mate Mutale Nalumango was also sworn in as vice-president.\n\nPresidents from Kenya, Botswana, Zimbabwe, Namibia and Tanzania were among several African leaders attending the inauguration ceremony at the National Heroes Stadium in the capital.\n\nWhen Hichilema, 64, came into office in 2021 after a landslide victory, the copper-rich nation had recently defaulted on its international debt and had an annual inflation rate of more than 24%.\n\n\"We inherited a broken-down countryâ¦ the economy was declining,\" Hichilema told the BBC in Lusaka before the election.\n\nThe former businessman negotiated a restructuring of the debt and rebuilt relations with foreign creditors. Inflation has also come down, but prices are still rising and many continue to struggle with the cost of living.\n\nHichilema now wants to build on the foundations he said he laid over the past five years.\n\n\"We are moving from stabilisation to growth,\" the president said in his inauguration in speech.\n\n\"Having done the heavy lifting in our first term, the next five years is all about growth and prosperity for all our people. I will continue to be a servant president for all the people of Zambia,\" he added.\n\nWith a recent rise in the price of copper - which the World Bank says accounts for about 15% of GDP and more than 70% of exports - investors remain confident and appear ready to overlook post-election issues, the Bloomberg news agency reports.\n\nMore on Zambian politics:\n\n- Ex-Zambian minister killed in security raid on opposition\n\n- Published20 August\n\n- Stone-throwing anger unnerves Zambia's 'fix-it' president\n\n- Published27 November 2025\n\nGo to BBCAfrica.com, external for more news from the African continent.\n\nFollow us on Twitter @BBCAfrica, external, on Facebook at BBC Africa, external or on Instagram at bbcafrica, external",
